<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sumptuary-law">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumptuary law</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sumptuary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sumptuary law is a law that restricts consumption, such as clothing or luxury goods, often according to social rank. Merchants were subject to sumptuary restrictions. These reflected the Confucian view that merchants ranked below farmers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law.jpg" alt="Sumptuary law" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abraham Bosse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bosse_Edict_1633.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The term comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sumptuariae leges. Black's Law Dictionary defines such laws as made &quot;for the purpose of restraining luxury or extravagance, particularly against inordinate expenditures for apparel, food, furniture, or shoes&quot;. Societies used them to limit the market for expensive imported goods and so regulate the balance of trade, to make status and privilege easy to see and so stabilise social hierarchies, and to reduce chances for political bribery. They often kept commoners from imitating aristocrats and could be used to stigmatise disfavoured groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The 7th-century BCE code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaleucus">Zaleucus</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a>, the first written law code of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, barred free-born women from gold jewellery or purple-bordered garments unless they were courtesans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> forbade extravagant funerals, the censors listed those found guilty of luxurious living in the nota censoria, and at the height of the Empire wearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple</a> without right was treason punishable by death. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> such laws existed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> onward; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, regulated graves by rank in 1368 and tightened the rules in 1396.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1603–1868) every class was bound by strict rules on dress, and in the 18th and 19th centuries these sought to keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> above the chōnin merchants, who had grown far wealthier. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, secular laws keeping people dressed according to their station began in the later 13th century, falling hardest on women and the middle classes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s first comprehensive act came in April 1463, and the Caps Act 1571 made males over six wear woollen caps on Sundays and holidays. In the Massachusetts Bay Colony a rule of 1634 reserved lace and gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> thread for the wealthy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Such laws were frequently ignored. The historian Lorraine Daston called them &quot;an extreme case of rule failure&quot;, and Montaigne argued that forbidding turbot and velvet to the people only made everyone more eager for them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> thought it &quot;the highest impertinence and presumption&quot; for kings and ministers to restrain private expense. The term later became a pejorative for any control of consumption on moral or health grounds: in 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a> decried prohibition in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as a bad sumptuary law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sumptuary law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A merchant class existed but ranked legally below farmers in the Confucian hierarchy and was subject to <b>sumptuary</b> restrictions and periodic state predation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sumptuary laws</b> kept fine clothing and jewellery for nobles and proven warriors, and taking captives in war was a commoner’s chief road to honour.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sumptuary laws</b> kept them to plain white clothes of cotton, and they wore straw shoes or wooden clogs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li><li><blockquote>Merchants stood last in theory, distrusted for piling up wealth, and the early Ming <b>sumptuary</b> laws barred them and their families from wearing silk; it was frowned upon for merchants to sit the examinations.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li><li><blockquote>Noblewomen dressed in long embroidered silk with trailing cloaks and furs, and <b>sumptuary</b> laws from 1488 and, from 1514, a magistracy watching spending on parties and clothes tried to curb the expense.</blockquote><a href="/societies/venice">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</a></li></ul></section>
